page cmd_console {
  title = "vmlab console"
  chapter_header "vmlab console" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-11-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab console` attaches a viewer to a VM's display. Machines on
    vmlab-hv use `vmlab-hv-viewer`, which connects to their native display
    socket. QEMU machines use an external VNC viewer or a localhost TCP
    bridge. It is the interactive
    counterpart of the screen verbs described in
    [Screens, input and vision](guide_vision).
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab console [OPTIONS] <VM>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<VM>`" | "The VM, as `[lab/]name`. A bare name is looked up in the lab of the current directory." |
      | "`--tcp`" | "Forward the VNC display over TCP instead of launching a viewer." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The command requires the lab daemon to be running already and never
    starts one: a stopped lab is refused with `lab \"<name>\" is not running`.
    Machine status supplies the actual hypervisor, also reported as `qemu`
    or `vmlab` in `vmlab status --json`. An unknown or stopped machine is
    refused. Containers have no display, so this verb is for VMs only.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A running template build's VM is reachable the same way, as
    `build-<arch>-<name>/build`; `vmlab template status` prints the
    reference (see [vmlab template](cmd_template)).
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    For vmlab-hv, the command passes `--display <display.sock>`,
    `--lab-socket <control.sock>`, and `--machine <name>` to the native
    viewer. The viewer waits for the display socket and reconnects after
    guest reboots. Closing its window leaves the VM running. `gui = true`
    opens the same viewer on `vmlab up`.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The native binary comes from `host.vmlab_hv_viewer` when set. Otherwise
    vmlab looks for `vmlab-hv-viewer` beside the configured `host.vmlab_hv`,
    then on `PATH`. A missing native viewer prints a one-line installation
    notice and falls back to VNC. Install the viewer from
    the vmlab-hv release bundle built from `hv/`; it is a separate binary. The optional
    `host.viewer_release_chord` is passed as `--release-chord`, for example
    `ctrl_r+alt_r`. Without it, the viewer uses left Ctrl+Alt.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    For QEMU and the VNC fallback, the viewer comes from the `viewer` command template in the
    [host configuration](guide_host) when one is set. Otherwise the command
    looks on `PATH` for `remote-viewer`, then `gvncviewer`, then `vncviewer`.
    A configured viewer is handed the socket path directly. A detected
    viewer needs TCP, so the command spawns a detached helper that holds a
    unix-to-TCP bridge open, launches the viewer against it, and prints
    `opened <lab>/<vm> in a viewer (closes with the window)`. The terminal is
    free straight away, and the bridge ends when the viewer window closes.
    This path requires the VM's `vnc.sock` to exist.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    With `--tcp` on either hypervisor, or when no viewer is found, the command binds a localhost
    port itself, preferring a VNC display port from 5900 upward so a client
    that takes `host:display` works, and prints
    `VNC for <lab>/<vm> on 127.0.0.1:<port>`. It then holds the bridge until
    Ctrl-C. This supports a Windows-side VNC viewer under WSL 2.
    The native viewer runs under WSLg and selects its shared-memory mode
    itself, without a TCP bridge or a `--shm` argument from vmlab.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The viewer shows the frame the guest puts on its scanout. On a Windows
    `vulkan` guest that frame can hold bands of stale pixels, which RDP into
    the guest does not show; see [Troubleshooting](apx_troubleshooting).
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab console winsrv
vmlab console --tcp mixed-lab/winsrv
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 after launching the viewer or ending the foreground
    bridge. Daemon, status, socket, and viewer-launch errors produce a
    nonzero exit status and a diagnostic on stderr. A detached viewer's
    later failure does not change the CLI's exit status.
  DOC
}
